+# Privacy engineering and deployment
+
+## Scope and audit result
+
+Source review on 2026-11-08 found a static HTML site with no client-side JavaScript, forms, analytics, cookies, service workers, browser storage, or authentication backend. The only third-party resource was the hero image from Unsplash. It is now a checked-in JPEG served locally, retaining the existing appearance without sending visitors' IP addresses or referrers to the image provider. System fonts need no font provider. No runtime dependencies or new third-party integrations were added.
+
+Image source (downloaded at development time, not by visitors):
+https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1350&q=80
+
+The Privacy footer link now resolves to `/privacy-policy/`. The static site's unimplemented Logout link was removed: there is no session to terminate here. Other existing navigation destinations remain outside this implementation.
+
+## Enforced defaults
+
+- No personal data is collected by page code. There are no scripts; CSP uses `script-src 'none'`, blocks connections, workers, embeds, objects, and forms, and allows only same-origin images and styles.
+- Inline styles were moved to a local stylesheet so CSP needs no `unsafe-inline`. The HTML meta policy provides a fallback on generic static servers. `frame-ancestors`, Permissions Policy, and other response headers require host support; they cannot be provided by that meta fallback.
+- Referrers and speculative DNS prefetch are disabled. Unused powerful browser features are denied. Frame protection, MIME sniffing protection, opener protection, and same-origin resource protection are configured in `_headers`.
+- HSTS starts at 300 seconds, without subdomain or preload commitments. Once production HTTPS is confirmed stable, the operator should raise its duration. HSTS is ignored over HTTP.
+- No violation reporting service is configured: there is no receiver, and reports may contain sensitive page URLs. Do not add telemetry without a purpose, sanitization, access controls, and deletion schedule.
+
+## Build and hosting
+
+Run `npm run build`. It performs the source privacy audit and copies only the approved inventory in `scripts/site-files.mjs` into `dist/`, including `_headers`. It does not start a server. `npm run check` runs the audit alone.
+
+`netlify.toml` configures Netlify to build and publish `dist/`; post-processing is disabled to prevent automatic resource rewrites. Netlify applies `_headers` to its static responses. Hosts with compatible `_headers` support can use the same output. On other hosts, translate `_headers` into their response-header configuration and serve only `dist/`. The existing `start` command is a development convenience, not a production security-header implementation. Do not assume an arbitrary static server interprets `_headers`.
+
+References: [Netlify custom headers](https://docs.netlify.com/manage/routing/headers/), [MDN CSP](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy).
+
+## Data handling outside the static code
+
+The hosting provider necessarily sees network request information. Hosting access logs, CDN analytics, retention, and injected platform scripts cannot be audited from this repository. Before deployment, disable optional host analytics and script injection, minimize access logs, omit query strings and credentials, redact identifiers such as IP addresses where possible, restrict log access, and configure the shortest operationally necessary retention and automatic deletion. Confirm those settings before making stronger public claims about hosting logs.
+
+If account, rewards, checkout, or contact services are implemented later:
+
+- Document purpose, recipients, retention, and deletion before adding fields. Collect only information needed for that action, support guest ordering, and never repurpose transaction data for marketing without separate consent.
+- Keep secrets and personal data out of URLs, client logs, localStorage, and IndexedDB. Validate and authorize on the backend; use HTTPS and `__Host-` session cookies with `Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Path=/` and no Domain attribute.
+- Handle logout server-side using a CSRF-protected state-changing request, invalidate the session, return `Cache-Control: no-store` and `Clear-Site-Data: "cookies", "storage", "cache"`. Do not apply data-clearing headers to public pages. Authenticated responses also need `no-store`.
+- Provide access, correction, and deletion alongside account creation. Use retention jobs to delete data when its stated purpose ends.
+
+## Repeatable third-party review
+
+Run the audit on every change and build. It guards this small site's approved markup, CSS resources, image signature, CSP consistency, and response-header defaults. It is a source regression check, not a general sanitizer or proof of deployed behavior. Any new published files must be added to the explicit publish inventory and reviewed.
+
+Before adding a dependency, embed, font, analytics SDK, or reporting service, record its owner, purpose, outbound fields, cookies/storage, subprocessors, retention/deletion, and update policy. Prefer local resources and static links; require an explicit user action for any necessary external embed. Version and integrity-pin any approved immutable external code, and revise CSP narrowly only after review. Reassess vendors at integration changes and at least quarterly.
+
+No browser checks or live server checks were performed for this task. Production response-header delivery, host-injected resources, and actual network behavior still depend on the deployment environment.
